# Engine telemetry — API.md §`GET /api/v1/telemetry`
#
# The whole engine state as JSON in one call: ruleset, lifetime counters,
# per-stage latency totals, upstreams, cache and memory. Replaces polling
# /cache and /debug/memory separately.
#
# Stable contract: new fields may be added, existing ones never change meaning
# or units. Figures that may change with the implementation live under
# /debug/* instead — see the boundary below.

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Everything. Blocks: process, ruleset, counters, latency, upstreams, cache,
### memory.
GET {{base}}/telemetry
{{auth}}

### Auth is required, like every /api/v1 route — expect 401.
GET {{base}}/telemetry

# ─── Reading the response ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY counter is process-lifetime cumulative. Charting a rate means deltaing
# two reads — and checking `process.uptime_seconds` first, because a restart
# returns all of them to zero and a delta across that boundary is meaningless,
# not merely small.
#
# JSON has no equivalent of Prometheus's `# TYPE`. The one field that is a
# LAST-VALUE GAUGE rather than a total is:
#   counters.cache_cleanup.last_duration_micros
# Deltaing it produces nonsense.
#
# `latency` gives `count` and `sum_seconds`, never an average: a lifetime mean
# flattens within hours of uptime. Delta both and divide for the interval mean.
# Percentiles need buckets — `history.http` serves them, windowed.
#
# counters.dns.cache_hits + cache_misses == pass + allow, never + block: a
# blocked query never reaches the cache (ADR-0001). Divide a hit ratio by the
# resolved queries, not by every query, or the ratio falls as the blocker
# improves.
#
# counters.dns.answers counts what the CLIENT saw, on its own axis:
#   servfail_synthesized  every upstream failed and no stale entry covered it,
#                         so FastAdHunter minted the SERVFAIL itself
#   servfail_relayed      an upstream answered SERVFAIL; passed through
#   refused_relayed       an upstream answered REFUSED; passed through
# A PARALLEL AXIS, NOT A PARTITION: each of those queries is also counted under
# pass/allow and under cache_misses, so the identity above still holds. Adding
# the three to pass+allow+block double-counts.
#
# A stale serve that masked an upstream failure is NOT here — the client got
# records, so it is counted by cache_stale. Reading these three as "outage
# minutes" therefore understates the outage and overstates the harm: that is
# the point of the split.
#
# Per-interval history of the same three figures is history.http's
# `fields=answers_delta`; there is no per-query outcome on any endpoint.

# ─── The producer boundary ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What lands here vs on /debug/* is decided by WHO PRODUCES a figure, not by
# how useful it looks:
#
#   FastAdHunter's own counters, ruleset, latency, upstreams, cache → here
#   Kernel: process_rss, process_peak_rss, major/minor page faults    → here
#   The linked allocator: allocator_committed_*                       → /debug
#
# The kernel figures come from getrusage and stay readable whatever allocator
# is linked in, which is why the stable surface may promise them.

# The allocator fields live in debug.http; the cache block is also served
# standalone by cache.http.
